<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="thiamine pyrophosphate biosynthesis IV (eukaryotes)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6908" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: THIAMINE-PYROPHOSPHATE &quot;Thiamine diphosphate&quot;|, also known as vitamin B1, is known to play a fundamental role in energy metabolism. It is an essential cofactor for a variety of enzymes such as |FRAME: EC-2.2.1.1|, |FRAME: EC-1.2.4.1|, |FRAME: EC-4.1.1.1|, and |FRAME: EC-1.2.4.2| |CITS:[15326535]|. Its discovery followed from the original early research on the anti-beriberi factor found in rice bran. Beriberi, a neurological disease, was particularly prevalent in Asia, where the refining of rice resulted in the removal of the thiamine-containing husk |CITS: [Begley96]|. Thiamine is synthesized &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; by microorganisms, plants and some lower eukaryotes (e.g. |FRAME: TAX-5820|), but not by higher eukaryotes, which must obtain it through their diet. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; This pathway describes the last part of the eukaryotic thiamine biosynthesis pathway - the coupling of the two moieties of thiamine, the thiazole and the pyrimidine, forming |FRAME: THIAMINE-P|, and its phosphorylation to the biologically active form, |FRAME: THIAMINE-PYROPHOSPHATE|. While most bacteria achieve this phosphorylation in a single step, catalyzed by |FRAME: EC-2.7.4.16| (|FRAME: G6234|) (see for example |FRAME: PWY-6893|), eukaryotes lack that enzyme and instead utilize a pathway where |FRAME: THIAMINE-P| is first dephosphorylated to |FRAME: THIAMINE|, which is then diphosphorylated to |FRAME: THIAMINE-PYROPHOSPHATE| (see |FRAME: THISYNARA-PWY|) |CITS: [19888457]|. In |FRAME: TAX-3702| the first step of the pathway is catalyzed by the |FRAME: AT1G22940| gene product, a multifunctional, chloroplast-localized protein capable of catalyzing several sequential steps in thiamine (vitamin B1) biosynthesis |CITS: [17174261] [16666289]|. The next step iscatalyzed by |FRAME:EC-3.1.3.100|. Despite earlier work that associated a different protein with this activity |CITS: [16666289]|, it is now accepted that the enzyme is encoded in |FRAME: TAX-3702| by the |FRAME: GQT-4007| gene |CITS: [26537753][27677881]|. The last activity of the pathway, |FRAME: EC-2.7.6.2|, is catalyzed in &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis&lt;/i&gt; by two similar cytosolic proteins, |FRAME: AT1G02880| and |FRAME: AT2G44750| |CITS: 17611796|. The subcellular compartmentalization of thiamine biosynthesis is a subject of on-going investigation. While the Arabidopsis proteins TH1, ThiC, and TH1, which catalyze the early parts of the pathway, all localize to the chloroplasts |CITS:[18048325][18332905][17174261][11575719]|, the downstream TPK1 and TPK2 enzymes are both cytosolic in Arabidopsis |CITS:[17611796]|, suggesting that intermediates in the vitamin B1 biosynthesis pathway may need to be transported out of organelles for TPP synthesis to be completed.</Description>
